from pathlib import Path
work = Path('work/boxed-world-review')
text = '''# Boxed solo-world native restoration gate

Frozen test-only candidate: `c95c008d22c9b7351463aa1575bf02347659d999`, parent `af060d8377389bb0b73de0e09d8f6de58b4f4c3f`, isolated branch `codex/hub-planar-shadows`. Only `crates/game_core/src/co_op_tests.rs` changes: one actual native regression plus two private fixture/probe helpers, 277 added test-only lines. No runtime, protocol, save, browser, renderer, service, listener or build-bundle changes. Root `5e4b629e91f3334582ffa30d5e65af98675f8c39` remains the reference live runtime; the Ion Wake `87de1f7` candidate is not in this test.

**Outcome:** no restoration runtime defect reproduced. The existing Box<Game> implementation preserves the sampled rich solo world and deterministic continuation across native End, host Leave and Home. The new gate adds genuine ECS/transient coverage missing from the older profile/player-oriented restoration test. This is native/source evidence; live controller teardown, browser state transitions and IndexedDB remain separate gates.

## Actual ownership and source contract

`game_core/src/co_op.rs:344` validates admission before mutation. It creates a fresh temporary host Game and copies the profile, viewport, quality settings and acquisition generation; the current solo Game is moved with `Box::new(std::mem::replace(self, active))` into `Session.solo`. Its `hecs::World` allocation, entity generations/components, profile, RNG and cached/transient fields belong to the box, not the temporary session. The session constructor explicitly restores the profile clone after Game::new migration, preventing temporary construction from discarding original cargo.

The Game owns World/player, RNG, camera/previous camera, time/expedition, paused/navigation/throttle, firing/wake/exhaust/repulsor/spawn/boss timers, hit timers/hull/shield, active boss/kill credit, particles/lights/events, chunk entity membership, wake samples, dock body, viewport/site cache, run summary, diagnostics, quality flags and material-loss notice. End and exact host Leave replace `self` with `*session.solo`. Home returns immediately after restoration instead of performing ordinary cargo discard, cleanup or dock repair. Ordinary guest Leave removes the exact Pilot and its epoch-owned shots only in the temporary authoritative World, invalidates publication and leaves the boxed solo Game intact. Invalid session/membership operations do not restore the wrong world.

**Intentional exception:** `restore_co_op` preserves the temporary session's advanced `publication_generation`. Acquisition IDs must not be reused. Thus “whole object restoration” is not a claim of byte-identical Game memory or every scalar: this monotonic generation differs intentionally. The old standalone publication cache is restored with the Game; the admitted fixture has no solo publication cache. No Profile snapshot can prove ECS/free-list/RNG restoration by itself.

The guest boundary differs from the host. `game_web/src/co_op_replica_bridge.rs:63/128` takes **&Game**, stores only a separate `Option<GuestView>` and never calls Game.tick or accepts a Profile. Pending/staged/committed body data and the presentation clock belong to Replica; valid replica_end disposes that view. Wrong-session, malformed, oversized and invalid/regressing-clock requests preserve ownership. Source-backed immutability applies to these native bridge calls. It is not a test claiming all surrounding browser settings are immutable.

`browser.rs` suppresses solo simulation, Profile emission and event draining while GuestView exists, but calls `update_world_viewport` each step and exposes setters for reduced motion/other presentation settings. Host/guest transitions reset accumulator, pending click and UI animations. The Root5e shell also intentionally resets controls/cancels routes, blocks solo saves/actions during co-op, and pauses blocked admission. `CoopController.#restore` ends native host authority or replica ownership, retains a restore_failed barrier on error and clears transient network/cache state. The source pins preserve the exact Root5e main.ts separately: our checkout has an older main.ts, although all thirteen other audited runtime boundary/core files are canonical-byte identical to Root5e.

## New actual regression

`co_op_tests.rs:934`: `boxed_solo_world_restores_ecs_transients_and_deterministic_continuation_after_teardown` runs three complete native scenarios. Two independently initialized identical solo Games form the pre-state and reference. The fixture actually streams asteroids/chunks, spawns a damaged segmented Coil enemy with four linked components, a real projectile retaining previous_hit, and a cargo/loot entity. It contains a damaged player body/hull, velocity/previous pose, live run summary/cargo, wake samples and timers, particles and a transient shield light, camera interpolation, RNG, event queue and nonzero combat/control timers. It equips ordinary Ion Wake in the solo Profile so resumed native ticks genuinely produce wake samples/events.

The semantic probe compares every basic ECS component created by this fixture (entity ID, Motion, Actor, complete PixelBody Debug field including occupancy/colors/surface map, Brain, Segment, Bullet, Loot, AsteroidBirth), sorted chunk/entity membership and the sampled private transient fields, exact serialized Profile/HUD/events. This is not a raw-memory hash and does not inspect uninitialized padding. Archetypes not created by this fixture (special biome AI/BossEncounter/arena etc.) are covered only by move ownership/source reasoning, not falsely advertised as exercised here.

Each scenario rejects an invalid protocol before admission; runs a real host plus guest for thirty fixed ticks with firing/thrust; captures a real publication; perturbs temporary body/effects/camera; rejects stale End; leaves the guest; checks the private boxed rich state against the original; rejects the stale repeated membership; then restores with End, exact host Leave or Home. It checks the acquired monotonic generation survives, no session/publication remains for this fixture, and the full semantic state matches. It also compares the exact native FrameBuilder Debug packet at interpolation alpha .375 before and after restoration. This is portable native extraction with the ordinary fallback-home path, not a GPU pixel/screenshot comparison.

Both restored and untouched reference Games then execute ninety identical real solo fixed ticks. Every tick compares HUD and exact bits from one explicit native RNG draw; at the end all sampled ECS/transient state matches, with actual nonempty Ion Wake and ability-wake events. These are controlled native continuation fixtures, not ninety client network frames, transport packets or a physical-device soak. The free-list/allocator continuation is indirectly exercised by real native entity activity; no unqualified allocation-byte equivalence claim is made.

## Actual checks and retained attempts

- `co-op-final.log`: final frozen source, 23 passing co_op_tests, one explicit serializer exporter ignored; includes the new three-scenario regression. This is the selected native suite, not all game_core/workspace tests.
- `guest-bridge.log`: four existing actual native guest bridge tests pass, covering malformed admission, wrong-session/valid End, mutually exclusive host/guest authority and monotonic receipt-clock teardown. Their Game assertion is profile equality; whole guest Game ownership is source-backed by the immutable bridge signature, not a fabricated richer bridge snapshot run.
- `clippy-final.log`: strict `cargo clippy -p game_core --tests -- -D warnings` passes. `fmt-final.log`: owned rustfmt and diff-check pass.
- `native-first.log`: an initially unqualified --exact filter ran zero tests. It is retained and does not count as acceptance.
- `native-first-exact.log`: actual failure because the initial fixture assumed Game::new streamed chunks. The corrected fixture calls the actual update_chunks path.
- `native-second.log`: actual failure because the fixture expected UnknownParticipant after leave; the exact epoch membership API returns StaleMembership. The expectation was corrected. These two failures were author-fixture errors, not reproduced runtime restoration defects.
- `native-final.log`, `co-op-regression.log`, early `clippy-native.log/fmt.log/diff-check.log` preserve intermediate successful runs; the final co-op/Clippy/format logs above anchor the frozen 277-line source including the live run summary.

## Remaining live QA gates and integration handoff

Root can cherry-pick only `c95c008`. All thirteen audited runtime inputs are unchanged from Root5e. The source pins and canonical snapshots record the test-only source, every audited core/bridge/controller input and the older/new Root main distinction. No independent acceptance of this new regression has yet been claimed; Root should source-review it before integration.

The current live helper, per the actual read-only report from audio_review, observes acquire/entity/body/replica_commit but not native begin/join/leave/end or replica_begin/end, and lacks a stable pre/post local-world HUD/body/RNG observation. It also intentionally pauses/resizes/changes UI and resets controls. A live acceptance record therefore still needs actual successful teardown operation counters, session/epoch observation, a carefully defined native solo observation before/after excluding intended controller/settings changes, and separate originally persisted versus current native Profile/save digests. Guest decline/failure before replica creation, failure after staging, guest leave and host end should be distinct real-client cases. Settings/viewport/control changes during a guest run are not rollback promises.

IndexedDB migrations/open, concurrent save work, failed restoration retry and actual signaling/WebRTC end propagation are outside this native regression. There was no browser, server, GPU, RTC, full build, physical-device or SaveStore execution in this task. It does not close actual two-client/eight-client/TURN gates, guest Ion Wake appearance, or previously qualified shadow GPU/performance gates.
'''
